data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** The syntax is straightforward:

```csharp
Span<nint>  span  = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> uspan = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system each element is 8 bytes (on 32-bit it would be 4). No `unsafe` context is needed — assigning a `stackalloc` directly to a `Span<T>` is safe code. This has been supported since C# 7.2 / .NET Core 2.1 when `Span<T>` was introduced, and `nint`/`nuint` were added as first-class types in C# 9.
